// The money-illusion chart: growth of $1 in US shares, Dec 1965 to Dec 1981,
// drawn twice — as the account statement showed it (nominal) and as the
// supermarket experienced it (real). The whole argument of the surrounding
// section is the widening gap between the two lines, and the real line ending
// below the dashed $1 it started from.
//
// Construction: the SVG carries ONLY geometry (gridlines and the two
// polylines, vector-effect non-scaling so strokes stay 2.5px at any width);
// every piece of text is HTML absolutely positioned by percentages, so type
// never scales below the 11px floor on a phone — the trap inline SVG text
// falls into when a 720-unit viewBox renders 350px wide.
//
// Series identity per the house colour rule: gold = nominal (the brochure
// number), navy = real. Gold is #8A6520, not the brand gold — the brand hue
// measures 3.83:1 on white and fails as text; this one measures 4.82:1 and is
// indistinguishable at stroke weight, so line and label share one colour.
//
// Coordinates are percentages of the plot box, y-domain $0.50 to $2.75,
// emitted by the same pipeline as every other figure in the piece (monthly
// Shiller total return; real series deflated by CPI over the same months).
// Endpoints re-derivable with /data/rising-rates.py: nominal 2.527 (+152.7%),
// real 0.855 (-14.5%).
//
// The third, dashed line answers "so where should the money have hidden?" —
// a 10-year par Treasury rolled monthly at FRED's GS10 yield, coupon accrued,
// deflated by the same CPI: $100 kept $63 of purchasing power. Cross-checked
// against Damodaran's independent year-end construction, which compounds to
// ~$60 — same story, different vintage, disclosed in the article's soft-spots.
// Rolled one-month T-bills (Ken French's series, matching his published
// figures to 0.4%) ended at $97.50 — cash stood still; it appears as a caption
// fact rather than a fourth line.
import ToolBrand from '../ToolBrand.astro';

